WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:05.790
Most popular articles about
quantum gravity, they first say,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.480
oh, we all know that quantum
theory is not really, you know,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.394
doesn't go well together with
general relativity.

00:00:12.394 --> 00:00:17.297
And then they say, well, here are the two
main contenders:

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:20.034
it's string theory, and 
it's loop quantum gravity.  

00:00:20.034 --> 00:00:21.990
These were not so simple theories.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.789
But in reality, I mean, research
has really moved away from these.

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:31.646
There other ways to look
at the world that are maybe more fruitful,

00:00:31.646 --> 00:00:34.114
and therefore, you
know, maybe more correct.

00:00:34.114 --> 00:00:39.320
And now we are going back to, you
know, good old quantum field theory.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.123
Go back to kind of minimal ingredients.

00:00:44.123 --> 00:00:45.624
Is there anything new?

00:00:45.624 --> 00:00:47.125
Well, the answer is

00:00:47.125 --> 00:00:51.337
that it's not only extremely rich dynamics,

00:00:51.337 --> 00:00:55.189
and totally unexpected things that happen,

00:00:55.189 --> 00:01:02.760
but there is also kind of you can, you know, 
visible the mathematics that belongs with that.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.570
My name is Renate Loll. 
I am a professor of theoretical physics

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.681
at Radboud University in the
Netherlands.

00:01:12.681 --> 00:01:18.277
And my main interest is to try and
understand what quantum gravity is.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:23.032
Quantum gravity is really the fundamental theory, 
quantum theory

00:01:23.032 --> 00:01:26.868
underlying classical
general relativity.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.659
General relativity has given us like
black holes,

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:31.198
a dynamical universe

00:01:31.198 --> 00:01:34.915
with a big bang, gravitational waves,

00:01:34.915 --> 00:01:39.540
amazingly successful. Now, what I would say 
we have to go one step further.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:47.428
So the crucial thing that you want to have 
from the theory of quantum gravity is,

00:01:47.428 --> 00:01:50.222
it must be the case that you recover, you know,

00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:52.414
some of the properties of
the classical theory.

00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:58.855
Do you then see any evidence that
interactions happen over long distances?

00:01:58.855 --> 00:02:02.358
When you zoom out, you should get out 
something that's four dimensional:

00:02:02.358 --> 00:02:03.990
three space plus one time.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.310
The idea is really, to
understand quantum gravity from

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.660
very elementary principles that
we all know.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.622
The idea has a name, it's called Causal
Dynamical Triangulations,

00:02:18.622 --> 00:02:21.041
which just describes basically the
ingredients we are using.

00:02:21.041 --> 00:02:28.506
Causal refers to the fact that we put 
by hand a causal structure into it,

00:02:28.506 --> 00:02:30.102
a time flow.

00:02:30.102 --> 00:02:35.554
Causal structure is present, at the most microscopic level of
stuff, I'm just putting in.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.016
Dynamical means I'm paying attention to 
the dynamical character of geometry

00:02:40.016 --> 00:02:44.020
when I write down and solve the path integral,

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.022
because the path integral is a way, you know,

00:02:47.022 --> 00:02:50.321
to take a quantum superposition of curved space-times.

00:02:50.321 --> 00:02:53.250
And you need a handle on how do I describe
these space-times.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.130
And the triangulations just tell
you, you know, what I've done to

00:02:58.032 --> 00:03:00.826
make these spaces or space-times

00:03:00.826 --> 00:03:04.500
amenable to computation. You know, I,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.032
I put a triangular mesh
on them.

00:03:08.032 --> 00:03:09.792
Triangles have just,

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.713
they explore curved spaces in a
quite effective way.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.216
If you take kind of these basic
ingredients, and you know,

00:03:18.216 --> 00:03:20.009
tell your computer what to do.

00:03:20.009 --> 00:03:22.730
Ask the right questions, we so far,

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.557
are getting very interesting
results out.

00:03:26.557 --> 00:03:30.519
The real breakthrough was: there is a
universe that comes out.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.730
It seems to have a non trivial
shape, that was something that

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.270
one could relatively easily
understand, the overall shape of

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.223
the universe we saw.

00:03:39.223 --> 00:03:41.796
And it had a kind of blob shape.

00:03:41.796 --> 00:03:47.693
So when we looked at it, you know, in time,
so we could monitor things in time,

00:03:47.693 --> 00:03:52.276
you could see it, it wanted to start kind 
of in a point.

00:03:52.276 --> 00:03:55.182
And then the spatial universe kind of grew.

00:03:55.182 --> 00:04:00.752
And then after it later times, it
collapsed back to a point.

00:04:00.752 --> 00:04:05.900
So we were able to show that, indeed,
the behavior, you know, of this

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.450
quantum space, and that was
sitting in our computer,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:12.681
generated, according to the rules

00:04:12.556 --> 00:04:14.015
what we now called CDT

00:04:14.015 --> 00:04:17.643
on sufficiently large scales became a,

00:04:15.016 --> 00:04:19.061
looked like a four dimensional object.

00:04:20.062 --> 00:04:22.168
That had never happened before.

00:04:22.168 --> 00:04:26.260
This is a priori in these models is
absolutely, it's not a given at all.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:33.240
So the question is, if you have
a true quantum theory of gravity,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.284
can we go one step further?

00:04:35.284 --> 00:04:38.551
That's where my research is now going.

00:04:38.537 --> 00:04:43.833
To understand can we possibly predict 

00:04:38.912 --> 00:04:47.230
what the very early universe should have looked like?

00:04:48.212 --> 00:04:48.962
It's very interesting work,

00:04:48.962 --> 00:04:51.511
it's like the work of an experimentalist almost,

00:04:51.511 --> 00:04:54.636
you know, to tease out these properties.

00:04:54.636 --> 00:04:59.513
It's just so rich and so many things can happen and we've just scratched the surface

00:04:59.513 --> 00:05:02.993
of all the things Is that can happen. So it's just the beginning of an,

00:05:02.993 --> 00:05:06.792
I would say, probably a very interesting
and non-trivial story of,

00:05:06.792 --> 00:05:09.440
you know, where quantum gravity
maybe is going.

